\subsection{Literature, source context and scope}
The full catalogue body, including qualifications and historical source status, is preserved above. The original workshop report was inspected in the locally cached publisher PDF text. This attempt distinguishes the source question from any explicitly restricted model introduced below. Repeated records retain their own source IDs and do not become independent conjectures. Elementary results and attributed literature results are not claimed as new original-target progress.

\subsection{Testing first-fit on the actual light class}
This repeated question concerns the same primary definition: an arc $u\to v$ is light when $\{w:v\to w\to u\}$ induces an acyclic tournament. The attempted algorithm processes vertices in a fixed order and gives each vertex the first of two colors that leaves its color class acyclic, without recoloring.
Consider vertices $0,1,2,3,4,5$, with the following fifteen arcs (exactly one for each pair):
\[
01,04,05,12,20,25,30,31,32,41,42,43,51,53,54,
\]
where $ij$ denotes $i\to j$.
\begin{proposition}
This tournament is light and two-colorable, but first-fit in the displayed vertex order fails at vertex five.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The complete list of directed triangles, recorded as unordered triples, is
\[
012,024,034,035,125,235,245.
\]
This list follows by checking the twenty triples: a triple is cyclic exactly when each vertex has one outgoing arc within it. From the arc list, the neighborhoods of $01,04,05,12,20,25,30,31,32,41,42,43,51,53,54$ respectively have the following topological orders:
\[
(2),(3,2),(3),(0,5),(4,1),(4,3,1),(5,4),(),(5),(),(0,5),(0),(2),(2,0),(2).
\]
Every earlier listed vertex points to every later listed vertex in its neighborhood. Hence all neighborhoods are acyclic and the tournament is light.
First-fit places $0,1$ in color zero. Triangle $012$ forces $2$ into color one. Vertex $3$ joins color zero, since $\{0,1,3\}$ is acyclic. Vertex $4$ cannot join it because of $034$, and joins $\{2\}$ in color one. Finally $5$ creates triangle $035$ in color zero and triangle $245$ in color one, so both choices fail. Nevertheless $\{0,2\}$ and $\{1,3,4,5\}$ are acyclic: neither contains any of the seven listed triangles. They give a valid two-coloring.
\end{proof}
Thus lightness plus a two-colorability promise does not justify irreversible first-fit. The example is not a hardness proof and does not exclude recoloring or a special ordering.

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
The example was found by enumerating labeled tournaments on five and then six vertices; the search found none on five for this fixed first-fit failure condition and found the six-vertex mask 2105. Independent direct checks verified all arc neighborhoods, all twenty triples, the failed coloring sequence and the successful partition. No global two-color algorithm is obtained.

